            <h3 style="color:#f87171">Sunday &mdash; Man&rsquo;s Institution</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>No Bible commandment &mdash; tradition of Rome alone</li>
              <li>The Papacy claims Sunday as its mark of authority</li>
              <li>Enforced by civil law &mdash; union of church and state</li>
              <li>The image of the beast: U.S. enforcing Rome&rsquo;s decree</li>
              <li>The number 666 identifies the man of sin &mdash; Rev. 13:18</li>
              <li>Those who receive it drink of God&rsquo;s wrath &mdash; Rev. 14:9&ndash;10</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.07);border:1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.25);border-left:4px solid #ef4444;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 22px;margin:24px 0;">
          <div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#f87171;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;margin-bottom:8px;">Ellen G. White — What It Means to Receive the Mark</div>
          <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.18rem;font-style:italic;color:#fca5a5;line-height:1.68;margin-bottom:8px;">&ldquo;To receive this mark means to come to the same decision as the beast has done, and to advocate the same ideas, in direct opposition to the Word of God.&rdquo;</p>
          <cite style="font-size:0.78rem;color:rgba(252,165,165,0.6);font-style:normal;">— Ellen G. White, <em>Review &amp; Herald</em>, April 27, 1911; <em>SDA Bible Commentary</em>, vol. 7, p. 979.12; <em>Review &amp; Herald</em>, July 13, 1897</cite>
